---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { md as _md, mdp as _mdp, mdHtml } from "../lib/md.js";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Sources from "../components/Sources.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/diaspora.json";
import mig from "../data/migration.json";
const dests = Object.entries(mig.counts).sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]);
---
<Base
  title="Where the Name Went"
  eyebrow="Seven clusters on four continents"
  dek="Searching the migration indexes by country rather than by name turns up whole branches this archive had never seen — the largest of them in southern Brazil, where dozens of De Franceschi settled among the Veneto colonies of Rio Grande do Sul.">

  <section class="prose">
    <p>Every previous sweep in this project searched for people. This one searched for places, and it changes the picture: the surname is not thinly scattered across the world, it is <em>clustered</em>, and each cluster has a date and a reason.</p>
    <p>Read the in-law surnames rather than the given names. They say which Italy a family came out of, and often which decade, more reliably than anything the migrant wrote on a form.</p>
  </section>

  {d.clusters.map(c => (
    <article class="cluster" id={c.place.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-")}>
      <h3>{c.place}</h3>
      <div class="hdr">
        <b>{c.size}</b>
        {c.when && <span>{c.when}</span>}
      </div>
      <p class="wh" set:html={_md(c.where)}></p>
      <ul>{c.families.map(f => <li set:html={mdHtml(f)} />)}</ul>
      <p class="read" set:html={_md(c.read)}></p>
    </article>
  ))}

  <section class="prose" style="margin-top:46px">
    <h3>What this is not</h3>
    <p>It is not a family tree, and none of these clusters is claimed as kin to the Istrian line except where the page says so. <em>De Franceschi</em> was invented independently wherever an Italian-speaking clerk had to tell one Francesco's sons from another's — see <a href={u("/name/")}>The Name</a> — and a shared surname across four continents is exactly what that predicts.</p>
    <p>What it does show is that the two branches this archive can document, the Gallignana people and the Crikvenica people, moved inside much larger movements: the Istrians into Brooklyn and, after 1949, into displaced-persons resettlement; the Venetians and Friulians into Brazil, Michigan and, later, Australia.</p>
    <h3>The three best loose threads</h3>
    <p><strong>The Philadelphia family of 1954.</strong> Bosiljka, Brank, Jela and Mojuil Defranceski — Croatian names, arriving nine years after the war, in no Croatian register searched here.</p>
    <p><strong>Rotilio in Buenos Aires.</strong> Named in the 2012 reunion album as the eighth of eleven children of Antonio DeFranceschi of Gallignana, married to Albina Chinkopan, with two daughters. <a href={u("/the-eleven/")}>The eleven</a> showed the album gave him his sister's husband's surname; David's tree nevertheless carries the two daughters, and one of them has an Argentine baptism ark attached — <a href="#argentina">read below</a>, because its citation names a father who is not a de Franceschi. Argentina's arrival records are held by <a href="https://cemla.com/buscador/" rel="noopener">CEMLA</a> and are free to search — but the search sits behind a CAPTCHA, which this archive does not bypass. It is a twenty-minute job for a human being.</p>
    <p><strong>Broken Hill.</strong> Three Defranceschi graves in the New South Wales silver field and almost nothing else. <a href="https://trove.nla.gov.au/" rel="noopener">Trove</a> needs <em>no key at all</em> to search from a browser — this page said it did, and that was wrong — and searching it on 17 September turned up one line: <strong>«DeFranceschi Bros, 716 Chappie Lane, Broken Hill»</strong>, in the New South Wales Government Gazette of 17 June 1966. A business, at an address, in the right town. The newspaper columns <em>have</em> now been read — 170 articles across every spelling, harvested 27 September — and what they hold is <strong>not yet published here</strong>: the Trove key was issued on a written undertaking that the request would be metadata only, and whether this archive may quote an article is a question standing with David on <a href="/worklist/">work list item 156</a>. The finding that the columns are read, and the finding that they must not yet be quoted, are both recorded there.</p>
  </section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>{d.american_vitals.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={_md(d.american_vitals.lead)}></p>

  <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--terra);margin-top:24px">
    <div class="lab">The one that names the parish</div>
    <h3>{d.american_vitals.hit.heading}</h3>
    <p set:html={_md(d.american_vitals.hit.text)}></p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px"><strong>{d.american_vitals.hit.why}</strong></p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px" set:html={_md(d.american_vitals.hit.next)}></p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px">
    {d.american_vitals.clusters.map(c => (
      <div class="one"><h3 style="font-size:18px">{c.t}</h3><p set:html={_md(c.w)}></p></div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="prose" style="margin-top:20px" set:html={_md(d.american_vitals.manifests)}></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px" set:html={_md(d.american_vitals.source)}></p>
</section>
<Rule name="loom" label="Where they left from" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>{d.departure.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={_md(d.departure.lead)}></p>
  <div style="margin-top:20px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
    {d.departure.rows.map(r => (
      <div class="one" style={r.key ? "border-left-color:var(--accent)" : ""}>
        <div class="lab">{r.lab}</div>
        <p style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdHtml(r.text)}></p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="prose note" style="margin-top:22px" set:html={_md(d.departure.next_dep)}></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px" set:html={_md(d.departure.source_dep)}></p>
</section>



<Rule name="pomegranate" label="The village sweep" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>{d.naturalisations.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={_md(d.naturalisations.lead)}></p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <tbody>{d.naturalisations.rows.map(([k, v]) => (
        <tr><th style="min-width:15rem" set:html={_md(k)}></th><td set:html={_md(v)}></td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px" set:html={_md(d.naturalisations.source)}></p>

  <h2>{d.tacoma.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdHtml(d.tacoma.lead)}></p>
  <div style="margin-top:20px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
    {d.tacoma.rows.map(r => (
      <div class="one" style={r.key ? "border-left-color:var(--accent)" : ""}>
        <div class="lab">{r.lab}</div>
        <p style="margin-top:8px;white-space:pre-line" set:html={mdHtml(r.text)}></p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px" set:html={mdHtml(d.tacoma.source)}></p>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="Trove, 2026" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>{d.trove.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdHtml(d.trove.lead)}></p>
  <div style="margin-top:20px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
    {d.trove.rows.map(r => (
      <div class="one" style={r.key ? "border-left-color:var(--accent)" : ""}>
        <div class="lab">{r.lab}</div>
        <p style="margin-top:8px;white-space:pre-line" set:html={mdHtml(r.text)}></p>
        {r.conf && <p class="mono" style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--ink-3);font-size:11.5px" set:html={_md(r.conf)}></p>}
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px" set:html={_md(d.trove.source)}></p>
</section>

  <Rule name="arrows" label={d.placeholders.heading} />
<section class="blk">
  <p class="dek" set:html={_md(d.placeholders.lead)}></p>
  <div style="margin-top:20px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
    {d.placeholders.rows.map(r => (
      <div class="one" style={r.key ? "border-left-color:var(--accent)" : ""}>
        <div class="lab">{r.lab}</div>
        <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={_md(r.text)}></div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="prose note" style="margin-top:18px" set:html={_md(d.placeholders.close)}></p>
  <h3 style="margin-top:32px">{d.placeholders.table.heading}</h3>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={_md(d.placeholders.table.lead)}></p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px">
      <thead><tr>
        <th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 10px 6px 0">Pen</th>
        <th style="text-align:right;padding:6px 0 6px 10px">People hanging in it</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {d.placeholders.table.rows.map(([c, n]) => (
          <tr style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule)">
            <td style="padding:7px 10px 7px 0"><strong>{c}</strong></td>
            <td style="padding:7px 0 7px 10px;text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums">{n}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="prose note" style="margin-top:16px" set:html={_md(d.placeholders.table.close)}></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Read from David's live MyHeritage tree, family tree 4, on 16 September 2026. <strong>Nobody in these pens is claimed as a relative</strong>; they are recorded because the sorting is itself a finding.</p>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="Argentina" />

<section class="blk">
  {/* NOT id="argentina". The Argentina *cluster* above already takes that id
      from its place name, exactly as Britain's does, and two anchors of one
      name on one page means every /diaspora/#argentina link in the archive can
      only ever reach the first of them. The cluster is the one those links
      mean, so this section gets its own. */}
  <h2 id="argentine-pen">{d.argentina.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:10px" set:html={_md(d.argentina.lead)}></p>
  <div style="margin-top:20px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
    {d.argentina.rows.map(r => (
      <div class="one" style={r.key ? "border-left-color:var(--accent)" : ""}>
        <div class="lab">{r.lab}</div>
        <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={_mdp(r.text)}></div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="prose note" style="margin-top:18px" set:html={_md(d.argentina.close)}></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">Read from David's live MyHeritage tree, family tree 4, on 17 September 2026, with the FamilySearch citations it carries. The Santa Fe baptisms are indexed in <em>Argentina, Santa Fe, registros parroquiales, 1634&#8211;1975</em>; the images behind them have not been opened. <strong>Living people are named and never dated.</strong></p>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="Leaving" />

{/* 12 November 2026 (the page merges, PR B): /emigration/ ("Leaving") is this section now; /emigration/ redirects to /diaspora/#leaving.
    The text below is the whole of the old page, in its own order; its title, eyebrow and dek are the section's heading, label and lead. */}
<section class="blk" id="leaving">
  <h2>Leaving</h2>
  <p class="lab">314 migration records · 221 publishable</p>
  <p class="dek">Passenger manifests, naturalisation petitions and draft cards, searched across every destination country the family is known to have reached. One thread runs straight out of Gračišće to Brooklyn.</p>
</section>


<section class="blk">
  <h2>Gallignana to Brooklyn</h2>
  <p class="dek">Gallignana is the Italian name of Gračišće. Four people carrying this surname were born there and are recorded in New York.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p><strong>Joseph Defranceschi</strong>, born 12 March 1892 at Gallignana, was naturalised in New York on <strong>2 February 1912</strong>. When he registered for the draft in 1917 the clerk wrote his birthplace as “Frieste, Austria” — Trieste, which is almost certainly the port he sailed from rather than the village he was born in. The naturalisation file has it right.</p>
    <p>Ten years later, on <strong>9 September 1922</strong>, two more arrived together at Ellis Island. <strong>Antonio De Franceschi</strong>, born 18 February 1887, gave his last residence as <em>“G. Pedena”</em> — Gallignana in the district of Pedena, which is Pićan. <strong>Albina De Franceschi</strong>, born 14 April 1900, gave <em>“G. Gallig…”</em>. They were on the same manifest.</p>
    <p>Antonio was naturalised at Brooklyn nine days after landing, on 18 September 1922, and again in April 1929; his wife is recorded as Marta, then Martha. Albina was naturalised in New York on 5 April 1928, by then married to a Frank <strong>Schinkepen</strong>.</p>
    <p>And the village kept sending people. <strong>Dario Defranceschi</strong> was born at Gallignana on <strong>26 September 1937</strong> — son of <strong>Basilio Defranceschi and Maria L. Rovina</strong> — and died in Brooklyn on 29 January 1991. A household still in Gračišće in 1937, in the years the 1945 census would count as one of its remaining Defranceschi families, with a son who ended up in the same New York borough as the 1922 arrivals.</p>
    <p>Separately, <strong>Lidia De Franceschi</strong> was born at Pola on 3 April 1900 to <strong>Giovanni Defranceschi and Domenica Clagnan</strong>, and lived at Clifton, New Jersey. Clagnan is one of the Friulian in-law surnames that turns up repeatedly in the Istrian registers.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:26px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Recorded in</th><th>What the record says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{mig.istria.map(r=>(
        <tr><td>{r.name}</td><td class="m">{r.coll}</td><td>{r.events}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>
<Rule name="arrows" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Where the records are</h2>
  <div class="grid g4" style="margin-top:22px">
    {dests.map(([d,n]) => (
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{n}</div><div class="l">{d}</div></div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:28px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Collection</th><th class="m">Records</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{mig.collections.map(([c,n])=>(<tr><td>{c}</td><td class="m">{n}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:28px">
    <p>The shape is not what the family story predicts. <strong>Canada is second</strong>, on the strength of the 1881–1922 passenger lists and the 1919–24 immigration records, and no line in the received account mentions Canada at all. <strong>Australia is third</strong>, almost entirely through the Victorian inward, outward and coastal passenger lists and the Western Australian arrivals at Fremantle and Albany — which fits a family that reached Australia by sea in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries rather than after 1945.</p>
    <p>Two absences matter as much. <strong>Germany returns nothing</strong> — 334 records scanned, not one from a migration collection. <strong>South Africa returns nothing either</strong>, from 73 records, even though thirteen of the seventeen people spelling it <em>Defranceski</em> live there. Botswana has no indexed collection at all. Those routes are real; they are simply not in this index.</p>
    <p>And nothing yet comes from a <strong>Trieste departure list</strong>. Trieste was the Austro-Hungarian emigration port, and the Austro-Americana line sailed from it to New York, but its outward manifests are not in the collections searched here. That is the single most valuable gap left on the leaving side.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<Rule name="pomegranate" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What is not published here</h2>
  <div class="note">{mig.rule} Of {mig.total} records found, {mig.published} meet that test. The rest are Social Security, residence and public-records entries for people who may be alive, and they are excluded from the build rather than hidden in it.</div>
</section>
<Rule name="helm" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>{mig.conterosso.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">{mig.conterosso.lead}</p>
  <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent);margin-top:20px">
    <div class="lab">{mig.conterosso.correction.lab}</div>
    <p style="margin-top:8px">{mig.conterosso.correction.text}</p>
  </div>
  <div style="margin-top:16px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
    {mig.conterosso.rows.map(r => (
      <div class="one" style={r.key ? "border-left-color:var(--accent)" : ""}>
        <div class="lab">{r.lab}</div>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">{r.text}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="prose note" style="margin-top:22px">{mig.conterosso.close}</p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">{mig.conterosso.source_cr}</p>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="Findmypast, 2026" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>{mig.fmp.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdHtml(mig.fmp.lead)}></p>

  <ul class="setlist">{mig.fmp.sets.map(([n, w]) => (
    <li><span class="sn">{n}</span><span class="sw">{w}</span></li>
  ))}</ul>

  <div style="margin-top:24px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
    {mig.fmp.rows.map(r => (
      <div class="one" style={r.key ? "border-left-color:var(--accent)" : ""}>
        <div class="lab">{r.lab}</div>
        <p style="margin-top:8px;white-space:pre-line" set:html={mdHtml(r.text)}></p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="prose note" style="margin-top:24px" set:html={mdHtml(mig.fmp.honest)}></p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">{mig.fmp.source}</p>
</section>

<Sources ids={["familysearch","findagrave","album2012","arolsen"]} />
</Base>

<style>
  .setlist{list-style:none;margin:22px 0 0;padding:0;display:grid;gap:5px;
    grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr))}
  .setlist li{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:baseline;background:var(--panel);
    border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:3px;padding:9px 13px}
  .setlist .sn{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;font-weight:600;flex:1}
  .setlist .sw{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;color:var(--ink-3);white-space:nowrap}
  @media(max-width:820px){.setlist{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>

